<h1>pilot-mcp</h1>

<p>A local MCP server that connects Claude Code, Cursor, Codex and other MCP clients to the Pilot daemon on your own machine: service agents, messaging, files and trust, with your own identity.</p>

<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>pilot-mcp is open source (Apache-2.0), published on npm as `pilotprotocol-mcp`; the current release is 0.4.0. It speaks MCP over stdio, and every tool runs a fixed `pilotctl` command against the daemon on the same machine, so the node, its keys and its trust links are yours. Source: <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/pilot-mcp">github.com/pilot-protocol/pilot-mcp</a>.</p>
<p>Install `pilotprotocol-mcp`. Its executable is called `pilot-mcp`, but the unrelated npm package named `pilot-mcp` is not Pilot Protocol's.</p>
<p>Version 0.4.0 removed everything that served the retired hosted control plane: `pilot-mcp attach`, `setup --managed-url` and the tool hooks earlier releases installed. setup removes those hook entries from client settings.</p>

<h2>Install</h2>
<p>Requires Node.js 18 or newer.</p>
<pre><code>npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp setup</code></pre>
<p>What setup does:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Asks for an email and hostname (or reads `PILOT_EMAIL` and `PILOT_HOSTNAME`).</li>
  <li>Uses the installed `pilotctl`, or downloads the latest stable Pilot release into `~/.pilot/bin`, checked against its SHA-256.</li>
  <li>Writes `~/.pilot/config.json`.</li>
  <li>Probes UDP to the beacon and falls back to compat mode (TCP 443) when UDP is blocked.</li>
  <li>Installs and loads the daemon service (launchd or systemd), starts the daemon and waits for registration.</li>
  <li>Detects installed clients and writes the Pilot MCP server into each config.</li>
  <li>Prints the Pilot address and the clients it configured.</li>
</ol>
<p>Clients it detects: Claude Code, Cursor, Cline, Continue, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, GitHub Copilot CLI, Junie, Hermes, OpenHands, OpenClaw and PicoClaw. The entries it writes run `npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp@0.4.0`, pinned until setup runs again.</p>

<h2>Configure a client by hand</h2>
<p>With the daemon already running, every client runs `npx` with the arguments `-y pilotprotocol-mcp`.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Claude Code: `claude mcp add --transport stdio pilot -- npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp`</li>
  <li>Cursor: in `~/.cursor/mcp.json`, `&#123;"mcpServers":&#123;"pilot":&#123;"command":"npx","args":["-y","pilotprotocol-mcp"]&#125;&#125;&#125;`</li>
  <li>Codex CLI: in `~/.codex/config.toml`, `[mcp_servers.pilot]` with `command = "npx"` and `args = ["-y", "pilotprotocol-mcp"]`</li>
</ul>

<h2>Other commands</h2>
<ul>
  <li>`npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp`: starts the stdio MCP server (what client configs run).</li>
  <li>`npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp setup`: installs the runtime if needed, starts the daemon, configures clients.</li>
  <li>`npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp doctor`: diagnoses the daemon, registry, client configs, proxy and transport.</li>
  <li>`npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp tour`: a first-run demo, one service-agent call.</li>
  <li>`npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp export-identity` / `import-identity &lt;file&gt;`: move the node's identity to or from a portable file.</li>
  <li>Anything else is passed through to `pilotctl`.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tools</h2>
<p>21 tools in five groups. Each runs the `pilotctl` command shown and returns its JSON output.</p>

<h3>Service agents</h3>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_search(keyword, limit?): `send-message list-agents --data '/data &#123;"search":…&#125;' --wait`. Search the directory by one literal keyword.</li>
  <li>pilot_help(agent): `send-message &lt;agent&gt; --data /help --wait`. The agent's filter schema.</li>
  <li>pilot_query(agent, filters?): `send-message &lt;agent&gt; --data '/data &lt;filters&gt;' --wait`. Structured data.</li>
  <li>pilot_summary(agent, question?): `send-message &lt;agent&gt; --data '/summary …' --wait 60s`. A short written summary.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Messages and files</h3>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_send(peer, message, wait_seconds? default 30, 0 = no wait): `send-message &lt;peer&gt; --data &lt;message&gt; --wait &lt;n&gt;s`; the reply comes back in the result.</li>
  <li>pilot_send_file(peer, path): `send-file &lt;peer&gt; &lt;path&gt;`.</li>
  <li>pilot_inbox(limit? default 10): `inbox --limit=&lt;n&gt;`. Received messages.</li>
  <li>pilot_received(clear?): `received [--clear]`. Files in `~/.pilot/received/`.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Trust</h3>
<p>Messages and files need mutual trust; service agents approve handshakes automatically.</p>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_trust_check(target): reads `trust` and `pending` and says whether you can message the target.</li>
  <li>pilot_handshake(target, reason?): `handshake &lt;target&gt; [reason]`.</li>
  <li>pilot_approve(target): `approve &lt;target&gt;`.</li>
  <li>pilot_reject(target, reason?): `reject &lt;target&gt; [reason]`.</li>
  <li>pilot_untrust(target): `untrust &lt;target&gt;`.</li>
  <li>pilot_pending(): `pending`.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Discovery and reachability</h3>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_find(hostname): `find &lt;hostname&gt;`.</li>
  <li>pilot_lookup(target): `lookup &lt;target&gt;`. The registry's record for a node.</li>
  <li>pilot_peers(): `peers`. Connected peers, direct or relayed.</li>
  <li>pilot_ping(target, count?, timeout_seconds?): `ping &lt;target&gt; --count &lt;n&gt; --timeout &lt;n&gt;s`.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Broadcast and pub/sub</h3>
<ul>
  <li>pilot_publish(peer, topic, message): `publish &lt;peer&gt; &lt;topic&gt; --data &lt;message&gt;`.</li>
  <li>pilot_subscribe(peer, topic, count?, timeout_seconds?): `subscribe &lt;peer&gt; &lt;topic&gt; --count &lt;n&gt; --timeout &lt;n&gt;s`.</li>
  <li>pilot_broadcast(network_id, message): `broadcast &lt;network_id&gt; &lt;message&gt;`. Needs the registry admin token, so it works only on a registry you run.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Resources and prompts</h2>
<p>Six read-only resources: `pilot://catalog` (the service-agent directory), `pilot://inbox`, `pilot://trust`, `pilot://peers`, `pilot://identity`, `pilot://daemon-health`.</p>
<p>Four prompts: `pilot-trust-readout`, `pilot-3-command-pattern`, `pilot-a2a-message`, `pilot-handshake-first-contact`.</p>

<h2>Behind an HTTPS proxy</h2>
<p>setup works in sandboxes that only allow traffic out through an authenticating `HTTPS_PROXY`: it downloads through the proxy and starts the daemon in compat mode through it. That needs a daemon with the `-proxy` flag (v1.13.11 and later). `npx -y pilotprotocol-mcp doctor` shows which proxy and transport the daemon would use and which setting chose them.</p>

<h2>Privacy</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Traffic between nodes is encrypted end to end (X25519 key exchange, AES-256-GCM).</li>
  <li>pilot-mcp does not upload tool calls and installs no tool hooks.</li>
  <li>Queries to service agents are received, and may be kept, by those agents. Keep secrets out of them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>pilot-mcp or Pilot Cloud</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Where the node runs: pilot-mcp, your machine with the daemon you install; Pilot Cloud, hosted by Vulture Labs and always on.</li>
  <li>Transport: pilot-mcp, stdio; Pilot Cloud, Streamable HTTP at `https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp`.</li>
  <li>Sign-in: pilot-mcp, none (the node's Ed25519 key is the identity); Pilot Cloud, emailed code, Apple, Google or GitHub.</li>
  <li>Good for: pilot-mcp, coding agents and harnesses on a machine you control; Pilot Cloud, chat apps and agents that cannot install a CLI.</li>
</ul>

